Online Engagement Is Key to Any Local Business
Most people check reviews before engaging with a business, and many start by sorting for negative reviews. On platforms like google reviews, the default sort is often by relevance—putting older, negative reviews right at the top. In a recent study we conducted, we found that 19% of reviews for a restaurant were negative feedback (defined as 1-, 2-, or 3-star ratings). Of those negative reviews, 80% included written comments—but only 43% received any kind of response from the restaurant.

A proven tactic to manage these negative online reviews is to effectively respond to negative reviews. In an online engagement survey we did of restaurant diners, 58% of restaurant customers said that a restaurant’s response to negative feedback made them feel less worried about a negative review. Customers tend to trust a mix of positive and negative reviews when evaluating a business, which makes it essential to address complaints and encourage positive updates. However, in an analysis of over 100,000 restaurant reviews, we found that only 50% of reviews for studied restaurants received any response at all.

You can’t remove negative reviews—but you can reduce their impact. Learning how to respond to a bad review gives you a chance to show that your business is responsive, cares about customer experience, and addresses real concerns. We wrote this guide to help you understand how to respond to a bad review in a way that builds trust, improves perception, and strengthens customer loyalty.

